<BaseLayout title="FM Radio Board — Qwiic/STEMMA I2C" description="Open source I2C FM radio breakout board for the RDA5807 chip, Qwiic/STEMMA QT compatible, Arduino and CircuitPython support.">
  <p class="eyebrow">Breakout Board · OSHWA US002137</p>
  <h1>ScoutMakes FM Radio Board — Qwiic/STEMMA I2C</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    An I2C breakout board for the RDA5807 FM radio chip by RDA Microelectronics, supporting Arduino and
    CircuitPython, with RDS (Radio Data System) capability for station and song information.
  </p>

  <p>
    The board carries connectors supporting SparkFun's Qwiic and Adafruit's STEMMA QT I2C standards (JST SH
    4-pin), so it drops straight into either ecosystem. It ships with a 4-post header for breadboarding and a
    770&nbsp;mm 24&nbsp;AWG antenna wire.
  </p>

  <p>
    The board is open source with design files provided, designed and manufactured in the USA, and certified by
    the Open Source Hardware Association (OSHWA&nbsp;US002137). This is the first FM radio breakout that
    supports CircuitPython and is STEMMA QT/Qwiic compatible.
  </p>

  <ul class="spec-list">
    <li>Qwiic/STEMMA QT compatible — JST SH 4-pin connectors</li>
    <li>Runs on the I2C standard, default address 0x11</li>
    <li>Supports Arduino and CircuitPython</li>
    <li>3–5V DC compatible</li>
    <li>RDS data and a user menu provided via example code</li>
    <li>ENIG RoHS-compliant PCB</li>
    <li>Power LED (trace can be cut to disable for power saving)</li>
    <li>3.5&nbsp;mm headphone jack</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Example code exposes a full serial user menu: volume up/down, next/previous preset, scan up/down, direct
    frequency input, station status, mono/stereo mode, bass boost, mute/unmute, RSSI signal data, and soft reset.
  </p>

  <p><em>Headphones and STEMMA QT/Qwiic cables are not included. A short 770&nbsp;mm/30.31&nbsp;in wire makes a
  good antenna — typically 1/4 the wavelength of the tuned frequency.</em></p>

